  {
   "id": "TGuB8sjABwAJ",
   "from": "Brandon Hurr",
   "author": "27405c4b711a1080",
   "date": "2019-03-31T23:38:31Z",
   "subject": "Re: Question about plot_models",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I looked into it some. <div><br></div><div>Essentially, I think the problem comes down to the fact that the order of discrete axes need to be dictated ahead of time by the order of the factor that is fed into ggplot2. In this case, the factor is being created in the function and it is defaulting to the default, which is alphabetical. As far as I know, there is no way to change this order after the fact. </div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m happy to be wrong here, but I&#39;ve not seen it. </div><div><br></div><div>How to get around it? </div><div>1. email the author of sjPlot and ask what the best way is.</div><div>2. hack the function plot_models() and create your own version that outputs the dataframe prior to plotting so you can make the change</div><div><br></div><div>Let us know how you get on. </div><div><br></div><div>Brandon</div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sun, Mar 31, 2019 at 1:14 PM Cath Kids &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">cathki...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
